{"causes":["The aftermath of World War II and the need for a new international monetary order led to the creation of the International Monetary Fund.","The Bretton Woods Conference in 1944 brought together representatives from over 50 countries to establish a new global economic system.","The failure of the gold standard and the need for flexible exchange rates drove the development of the IMF's lending programs and policy advice."],"effects":["The IMF's establishment helped to stabilize international trade and finance, reducing the risk of global economic crises.","The IMF's lending programs have provided critical financial support to countries facing economic challenges, such as debt restructuring and crisis management.","The Bretton Woods system established by the IMF promoted economic growth and cooperation among nations, laying the groundwork for future international economic institutions."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"United States","sourceName":"United States of America","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"International Monetary Fund","targetName":"International Monetary Fund","context":"The United States played a leading role in the creation and initial development of the IMF."},{"sourceSlug":"Bretton Woods Conference","sourceName":"Bretton Woods Conference 1944","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"International Monetary Fund","targetName":"International Monetary Fund","context":"The Bretton Woods Conference established the IMF as a key institution for international monetary cooperation."},{"sourceSlug":"European Union","sourceName":"European Coal and Steel Community (ECSC)","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"International Monetary Fund","targetName":"International Monetary Fund","context":"The European Coal and Steel Community (ECSC) collaborated with the IMF to develop common economic policies and standards."},{"sourceSlug":"World Bank","sourceName":"International Bank for Reconstruction and Dvelopment (IBRD)","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"International Monetary Fund","targetName":"International Monetary Fund","context":"The World Bank collaborated with the IMF to develop and implement economic development programs."},{"sourceSlug":"G20","sourceName":"G20 Summit 2008","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"International Monetary Fund","targetName":"International Monetary Fund","context":"The G20 summit in 2008 featured the IMF as a key player in addressing global economic challenges."},{"sourceSlug":"United Nations","sourceName":"United Nations General Assembly","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"International Monetary Fund","targetName":"International Monetary Fund","context":"The United Nations General Assembly collaborated with the IMF to develop and implement global economic policies."},{"sourceSlug":"IMF Board of Directors","sourceName":"Board of Governors of the International Monetary Fund","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"International Monetary Fund","targetName":"International Monetary Fund","context":"The IMF Board of Directors succeeds to the position of Director-General, overseeing the institution's operations."},{"sourceSlug":"Bretton Woods Agreement","sourceName":"Bretton Woods Agreement 1944","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"International Monetary Fund","targetName":"International Monetary Fund","context":"The Bretton Woods Agreement established the IMF as a key institution for international monetary cooperation."}],"places":[{"name":"Washington, D.C.","role":"Headquarters"},{"name":"Bretton Woods","role":"Conference site"},{"name":"New York City","role":"Original location of IMF headquarters"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: International Monetary Fund","type":"Reference article","slug":"international-monetary-fund"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7804","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Monetary_Fund"],"tags":["United States","specialized agency of the United Nations","Americas","International Organizations","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"International Monetary Fund is remembered as a landmark institution of the Modern era, referenced in 157 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The International Monetary Fund (IMF) is an international organisation of 190 member countries established in 1944 at the Bretton Woods Conference — the landmark post-World War II financial summit in Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, that created the framework of the modern international monetary system. The IMF came into formal existence in December 1945 and began financial operations in 1947. Headquartered in Washington, D.C., the IMF's stated purpose is to foster global monetary cooperation, secure financial stability, facilitate international trade, promote high employment and sustainable economic growth, and reduce poverty around the world. It is one of the two major Bretton Woods institutions (along with the World Bank) that have shaped the global financial architecture since 1945.

The IMF operates as a lender of last resort for countries facing balance-of-payments crises — nations that cannot pay their international debts or maintain their currency's value in international markets. Member countries contribute quotas (proportional to their economic size) that form the Fund's lending resources. When a country requires IMF assistance, it negotiates a 'programme' — a package of loans conditional on economic reforms (known as 'conditionality') that the IMF deems necessary for financial stabilisation. The structural adjustment programmes that the IMF required of borrowing countries — particularly in the developing world during the 1980s and 1990s — typically included currency devaluation, fiscal austerity, trade liberalisation, and privatisation of state enterprises.

The IMF has been one of the most influential and contested institutions in modern international economics. Its advocates credit it with preventing financial contagion, managing debt crises, and promoting the market-oriented policies that underpinned the growth of the global economy. Its critics — from the left and the right — argue that IMF conditionality programmes have imposed unnecessary austerity on poor countries, undermined national sovereignty, and served the interests of creditor nations and international finance capital rather than the populations of debtor countries.","subjects":["International Finance","Global Institutions","Modern Era","Economics","Cold War","Modern History","Bretton Woods","Development Economics"],"frameworks":["WORLD_SYSTEMS","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The IMF's creation and development reflect the need for international cooperation and economic stability in the aftermath of World War II, shaping the global economic landscape and promoting cooperation among nations.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
